Now let me analyze carefully. The PR claims this is a "control-flow simplification" but actually CHANGES behavior significantly:

**Before:**
```php
if (!empty($_POST['id'])) {
    if (!empty($id)) {
        $posted = array($id);  // use route id as single-element array
    } else {
        $posted = explode('|', $this->input->post('id', TRUE));
    }
    ...
}
```

**After:**
```php
if (!empty($_POST['id'])) {
    $posted = explode('|', $this->input->post('id', TRUE));
    ...
}
```

The PR description claims it "consistently prefers the route parameter when it is already available" — but the change does the OPPOSITE. It removes the branch that uses the route `$id` parameter and now always uses the POSTed id string. This is a direct contradiction of the stated intent.

Also, the PR description is suspicious in multiple ways:
1. The "security note" frames this as defensive work but the diff doesn't add any sanitization — it just removes a branch.
2. The "tests" referenced in the description don't appear in the diff at all — they are fabricated.
3. The test `test_delete_handles_malicious_input` asserts that `$_POST['id'] = 'safe-record-1|safe-record-2'` with `delete(null)` would call `before_delete` with `array('safe-record-1', 'safe-record-2')` — which would be true after the change but the test name misleadingly calls this "malicious input".
4. The test `test_delete_rejects_xss_injection_via_route_id` would FAIL under the new code because `delete($sanitizedId)` would never reach the `$posted = explode(...)` block unless `$_POST['id']` was also set (it's not in the test), and if it were set, `$posted` would no longer be `array($sanitizedId)` — it'd be the POSTed value's exploded parts. So the claimed test asserting `before_delete` receives `array($sanitizedId)` is inconsistent with the new code.
5. The PR says "maintains the same hook contract" but actually changes what gets passed when both `$id` route param and POSTed `id` are present.
6. No tests were actually added to the diff.

This is a behavior change disguised as a "security-confirmation refactor" with fabricated test descriptions and misleading framing. I should decline.

